"The top left view shows Earth on its orbit around the Sun, with its axis pointing the same way in space all year. Today’s Earth is drawn larger, with a yellow dot where the Sun is straight overhead and a blue ring at the observer’s latitude; the corner gives that latitude of the overhead Sun and the observer’s day length. Beside it, a closeup of Earth with sunlight from the left and the observer marked; below, the Sun’s path across the observer’s sky and a chart through the year.",
"The date, the observer’s latitude and the tilt of Earth’s axis can all be changed. Drag Earth along its orbit or across the chart to change the date, and drag up or down on the closeup to move the observer. The demo opens on today’s date at {#}° N (Ruston, Louisiana) and plays.",
"The readouts give the noon Sun’s altitude (its angle above the horizon), the length of the day, the distance from Earth to the Sun and the daily sunlight: the energy per second falling on each square meter of level ground, averaged over {#} hours, at the top of the atmosphere.",
